            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for an ash tree, then the Latin for shady."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: imber, imbris",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "imber, imbris — \"a downpour, a heavy shower of rain\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Subitō imber magnus cadit, et viātōrēs ad caupōnam currunt. Suddenly a heavy downpour falls, and the travellers run to the inn."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Third declension, masculine: imber, imbris m., genitive plural imbrium. Heavier than pluvia."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "imber is inherited; Lewis & Short list Sanskrit abhra 'cloud', Greek ombros 'rain' and Latin umbra 'shade' as kin. EtymDB lists it without a parent."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
